    "leafs": {
      "full_name": "Toronto Maple Leafs",
      "league": "NHL",
      "record": "32-36-14",
      "detail": "8th in Atlantic Division",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Leafs",
        "body": "The season died by a thousand cuts, then one long final thud - <strong>Toronto is done, buried at 32-36-14, eighth in the Atlantic and carrying a seven-game losing streak into the summer</strong>. That's not a bad break. That's a collapse. A club with 78 points and 31 games in the cellar behind the leaders has nothing to argue with and plenty to answer for. The Maple Leafs spent too much of the year chasing structure they never quite found, and too many nights looking soft when the game tightened. The stars never turned the tide often enough, the middle of the lineup went missing for long stretches, and the back end leaked just enough to keep the whole enterprise from settling down. Now comes the hard part - no more excuses, no more hiding behind a hot week in November. Coaching questions will hang over the summer, free agency will bring the usual churn, and the front office has to decide whether this group needs a tweak or a teardown. <strong>What matters next is whether Toronto finally uses this wrecked season to make a real change, not a cosmetic one.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated May 20, 2026"
      },
