    "jays": {
      "full_name": "Toronto Blue Jays",
      "league": "MLB",
      "record": "21-27",
      "detail": "3rd in AL East",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "The Blue Jays keep chewing on the same hard truth: when the bats finally flare, the mound answers with smoke and ashes. <strong>Last night's 5-4 loss to the Yankees</strong> was the latest bruise, their second straight against New York and their 11th loss in the last 12 games, a slide that has pushed them to 21-27 and third in the AL East, 11.5 games out. That is not a bad week - that is a season slipping through the fingers. Toronto did put up a fight, and Ben Rice's two-run homer for the Yankees turned the fifth inning into the game's hinge, but the Jays could never fully seize control after squandering another chance in the Bronx. The bigger story is the drift: a club that looked tidy against Detroit now looks ragged, a team with too little margin and too many holes. At this stage, the Jays need a clean start, a hitter to carry the middle of the order, and a pitcher to stop the bleeding. <strong>Tonight's rematch at Yankee Stadium at 6:05 p.m. ET is a chance to arrest the slide before it becomes a habit</strong>.",
        "updated": "Updated May 20, 2026"
      },
